imagine you're getting ready to log off  for the day and you've just had an  urgent request an analyst at your  company can access the data they need  and will because you're the admin of the  cloud deployment they need your help  figuring out why they can't access the  data they need  your heart sinks a little because  debugging access issues can be so time  consuming but do they have to be let's  look at how a policy troubleshooter can  help you fix these type of issues fast  policy troubleshooter empowers admins  too easily and quickly understand why  specific actions are allowed or Not by  the IIM policies these actions could be  accessing resources like a storage  bucket or calling specific apis  in order to use policy troubleshooter  you need to provide just three pieces of  information the email address of a  specific user or service account the  resource in question and the permission  you can find this information in both  logs and in permission to night error  messages provided directly when users  experience access issues policy  troubleshooter takes this information  and examines the IAM policies that apply  to the resource to reveal whether any of  the user's existing rules include the  required permissions  if the user doesn't have adequate access  the troubleshooter lists the roles in  your IAM policy that contain the  permission in case you want to Grant the  user one of those roles  policy troubleshooter speeds up  debugging even in complex use cases such  as troubleshooting access to a resource  granted by a group or a nested groups or  understanding how policies are inherited  across the resource hierarchy  so going back to the original example an  administrator is troubleshooting a cloud  storage bucket access issue for a data  analyst let's resolve it using policy  troubleshooter first you'll head into  the policy troubleshooter tool  then provide the user or service account  email a resource and a permission  the full resource name reference  documentation is linked in the video  description  note that you can also add multiple  resource permission pairs to check at  one time  now click check access  the results page contains several pieces  of information evaluation details show  the summary of the principle resource  and permissions you are troubleshooting  the IIM allow policy section immediately  indicates whether the user has  permission to the resource  the resources section is a list of the  specified resource and its ancestors in  this case the project that owns the  bucket  however note that if you don't have  permission to view and ancestors allow  policies the resource list will not  include that ancestor  after selecting the project resource the  role bindings section populates A list  of rule Bindings that are currently  present in the project  by default this list only shows what are  called relevant bindings  relevant role bindings are role Bindings  that include a roll with that specified  permission or include a custom role that  can be edited to add the specified  permission  the access column indicates whether the  role has been granted to the user  if a role binding includes a condition  the access column will show as  conditional and The Binding details will  display the full condition  in this case the storage object viewer  binding is conditional on the day of the  week and the user's access is limited to  Mondays according to the central time  zone  in this demo we showed policy  troubleshooter using the Google Cloud  console but it's also available using  the Google Cloud CLI or the rest API for  simple queries like the one we showed  using the Google Cloud console is  typically fastest for more complex  scenarios consider the gcloud CLI or the  rest API we hope that policy  troubleshooter will help reduce the time  and the headache for anyone debugging  access issues in the cloud to learn more  about policy troubleshooter and the  other solutions that are part of the  policy intelligence portfolio check out  cloud.google.com policy intelligence
 

